{% block titre %}The Graduale Triplex{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Understanding the three superimposed scripts of the Graduale Triplex: Vatican square notation, neumes of Laon and neumes of St. Gall.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>The <em>Graduale Triplex</em>&nbsp;: three scripts for a single chant</h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    Why three stacked notations&nbsp;? Because none of them says everything on its own.
    Together they give both the right note and the soul of the chant.
</p>

<p class="lettrine">
    When the monks of Solesmes published the <em>Graduale Triplex</em> in 1979, they were
    answering an old problem. The oldest chant manuscripts (9th–11th century) are of
    extraordinary musical subtlety&nbsp;: they notate the nuances, the leanings, the
    impulses of the voice. But they have one major flaw&nbsp;: they <strong>do not give the
    exact pitches</strong>. Conversely, modern square notation gives precise pitches… but
    has lost much of those nuances. The solution&nbsp;? Superimpose the two worlds.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">The key word — diastematic</span>
    <p>
        A notation is <strong>diastematic</strong> when the height of the signs precisely
        indicates the pitch of the sounds (as on a staff). It is
        <strong>adiastematic</strong> (or «&nbsp;in campo aperto&nbsp;», in the open field)
        when the signs float above the text&nbsp;: they show the melodic <em>gesture</em>
        without fixing the intervals. The old neumes are adiastematic&nbsp;; square notation
        is diastematic.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>The three «&nbsp;storeys&nbsp;» of the page</h2>
<p>
    On a line of the <em>Graduale Triplex</em>, for each syllable of the text, you will find
    three pieces of information aligned vertically&nbsp;:
</p>

<div class="legende-triplex">
    <span><span class="pastille laon"></span>Above&nbsp;: neumes of <strong>Laon</strong></span>
    <span><span class="pastille vat"></span>In the middle&nbsp;: <strong>square notation</strong> (Vatican edition)</span>
    <span><span class="pastille sg"></span>Below&nbsp;: neumes of <strong>St. Gall</strong></span>
</div>

<h3>In the middle&nbsp;: the square notation (the Editio Vaticana)</h3>
<p>
    This is the reference layer for <strong>pitch</strong>. On its four-line staff, with its
    clef, it tells you exactly which note to sing. It is the one we learned in <a href="{{
    path('paleo_en_notation') }}">The basics</a>. If you were to read only one layer to
    «&nbsp;get out&nbsp;» the melody, it would be this one.
</p>

<h3>Above&nbsp;: the neumes of Laon</h3>
<p>
    They come from a 9th-century manuscript kept at Laon (the «&nbsp;manuscript 239&nbsp;»)
    and are printed <strong>in black</strong>. This family of writing, called
    <em>Messine</em>, is famous for its <strong>rhythmic</strong> precision&nbsp;: it
    distinguishes short from long notes very finely, notably thanks to added letters (see
    below).
</p>

<h3>Below&nbsp;: the neumes of St. Gall</h3>
<p>
    They come from manuscripts of the abbey of St. Gall (in present-day Switzerland), such
    as the <em>Cantatorium</em> (manuscript 359) or the Einsiedeln 121 manuscript, and are
    printed <strong>in red</strong>. This script is admired for the clarity with which it
    draws the <strong>gesture</strong> of the melody and its articulation, likewise enriched
    with small letters.
</p>

<figure class="facsimile">
    <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/triplex-exemple.jpg') }}" alt="Page of St Gall neumes from the St Gall Cantatorium, manuscript source of the neume tradition">
    <figcaption>The <em>Graduale Triplex</em> edition (Solesmes, 1979) remains under copyright and cannot be reproduced here. Instead, here is a genuine, freely licensed manuscript page standing at the origin of this neume tradition&nbsp;: the opening of the introit "Ad te levavi" in the St Gall <em>Cantatorium</em> (Stiftsbibliothek St Gallen, Cod. Sang. 359, p.&nbsp;25), 10th century — one of the oldest sources of St Gall neumes, digitised on e-codices (CESG). Source: Wikimedia Commons, public domain.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The «&nbsp;significative letters&nbsp;»</h2>
<p>
    A delightful detail&nbsp;: the old copyists added <strong>small letters</strong> beside
    the neumes to specify the intention — rather like our markings «&nbsp;softly&nbsp;»,
    «&nbsp;hold back&nbsp;» above a score. They are called <span class="mot-latin">litterae
    significativae</span>. Here are some of the most frequent&nbsp;:
</p>

<table class="grille">
    <caption>A few significative letters (usage varies between Laon and St. Gall)</caption>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Letter</th><th>Latin word</th><th>Meaning for the singer</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>c</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">celeriter</span></td><td>quickly, without dragging</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>t</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">tenete</span></td><td>hold, lengthen the note</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>a</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">altius</span></td><td>higher (go well up)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>i / iu</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">iusum / inferius</span></td><td>lower</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>l</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">levare</span></td><td>lifting, lightening</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>m</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">mediocriter</span></td><td>moderately (neither too much nor too little)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>e</strong></td><td><span class="mot-latin">equaliter</span></td><td>at the same pitch</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">How to use it in practice</span>
    <p>
        There is no need to decode everything at once&nbsp;! The comfortable method&nbsp;:
        first read the <strong>note</strong> in the square layer in the middle, then look
        above and below and ask yourself «&nbsp;should I press on, settle,
        lighten&nbsp;?&nbsp;». The two old layers often confirm each other — and when they
        diverge, that is precisely where the chant becomes fascinating to interpret.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">Key point</span>
    <p>
        <strong>Triplex = 3 layers.</strong> The centre (square notation) gives <em>the
        notes</em>&nbsp;; Laon (on top) and St. Gall (below) give <em>rhythm and
        expression</em>. We shall now see all of that come alive in six concrete chants.
    </p>
</div>

